  "theory": {
        "intro": "Surd form and index form are two ways of writing the same number. To translate, read off two things: the index of the root becomes the denominator, and the power of the radicand becomes the numerator.",
        "facts": [
          "$\\sqrt[n]{a}=a^{\\frac{1}{n}}$ — a root with no written power has numerator $1$.",
          "$\\sqrt[n]{a^{m}}=a^{\\frac{m}{n}}$ — the **root index is the denominator and the radicand's power the numerator**, so $\\sqrt[5]{x^{3}}=x^{\\frac{3}{5}}$, not $x^{\\frac{5}{3}}$.",
          "$\\left(\\sqrt[n]{a}\\right)^{m}=\\sqrt[n]{a^{m}}=a^{\\frac{m}{n}}$ — rooting then powering and powering then rooting give the same index form.",
          "A square root has an unwritten root index of $2$: $\\sqrt{x}=x^{\\frac{1}{2}}$.",
          "**Only what is inside the root gets the fractional index**, so $7\\sqrt{x^{5}}=7x^{\\frac{5}{2}}$ but $\\sqrt[5]{2x}=(2x)^{\\frac{1}{5}}$.",
          "A root in the denominator becomes a negative index: $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{y^{2}}}=y^{-\\frac{2}{3}}$, and reversing this puts the root back underneath.",
          "When the power divides exactly by the root index, the index simplifies: $\\sqrt[4]{y^{12}}=y^{\\frac{12}{4}}=y^{3}$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Write the index of the root as the denominator of the index.",
          "Write the power of the radicand as the numerator of the index.",
          "Leave any factor outside the root unchanged, and use a negative index if the root sits in a denominator."
        ]
      },
